*.pdf

# Source material stays on the machine that owns it. The PDFs are ignored by
# the line above; the licensed Darrington Press community logos are ignored
# here because the four the app actually uses are checked in under
# public/brand/ and the rest is a 5 MB asset pack this repo has no use for.
#
# Both spellings, for the reason spelled out under `node_modules` below - and
# this one is not hypothetical either. A parallel-lane session symlinked
# `Manuali` into seven worktrees so every lane would run the same suite this
# checkout runs, `Manuali/` did not match the link, and one lane's `git add -A`
# committed a 120000-mode blob pointing at this directory. Merging it would
# have replaced 321 MB of the owner's own purchased PDFs with a symlink to
# itself. It was caught before the merge; the slash is why it got that far.
Manuali
Manuali/
# And the same two spellings for the project-local Node toolchain, which the
# comment above and `env.sh`'s last paragraph both already claim is ignored.
# It was ignored as a directory only, and in a worktree it is not a directory:
# `env.sh`'s own setup block symlinks `.tools` back to the main checkout the
# same way `Manuali` and `node_modules` are symlinked, so `.tools/` did not
# match it and `git status` in every worktree listed the link as untracked -
# one `git add -A` away from the failure written up twice on this page.
.tools
.tools/
# Both spellings, and the one without the slash is the load-bearing one. A
# pattern ending in `/` matches a directory and nothing else, so when work is
# done in a git worktree - where `node_modules` is a *symlink* back to the main
# checkout, because npm installs once - `node_modules/` does not match it and a
# `git add -A` commits the link. That happened: the symlink rode a merge into
# the main checkout, git wrote it over the real directory, and every command
# that needed a dependency answered "too many levels of symbolic links".
node_modules
node_modules/
dist
dist/
coverage/
.DS_Store
*.local
.vite/
tools/.cache/

# Agent and developer scratch harnesses
audit-*.html
*-preview.html
!preview.html
.print-verify/
.design-verify/
.blueprints/

# WhatsApp source material from playtesters. Same rule as Manuali: it stays on
# the machine that owns it. What was extracted from it is written into BACKLOG.md.
# Both spellings again - `Giorgio*/` matches a directory and not a link to one.
Giorgio*
Giorgio*/

# Agent worktrees. The workflow runner checks a full copy of this repository out
# into each of these, so without this line a `git add -A` commits the tree once
# per lane - and each copy carries a `node_modules` symlink, which is the trap
# that has already overwritten the real directory once in this project. No
# trailing slash, for the same reason `node_modules` has none: a pattern ending
# in `/` matches directories only and a symlink is not one.
.claude/worktrees

# Per-user overrides by definition; the shared settings file is not this one.
.claude/settings.local.json
